  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/f/fb/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_103.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The issue picks up exactly where the last one left off, with Thor returning to the 20<sup>th</sup> century, fresh from his encounter with Zarrko, the Tomorrow Man.&nbsp; He returns to his office, changes to Don Blake, and collapses from exhaustion.&nbsp; Jane helps move him to a couch, all the while wondering why he doesn't propose marriage.\n\nMeanwhile, back in Asgard, Odin is still disappointed that he has not been able to get Thor to forget the mortal Jane Foster.&nbsp; Loki suggests that he only needs to meet a girl \"who is <i>more</i> beautiful, <i>more</i> desirable.\"&nbsp; Odin immediately realizes he's speaking of the Enchantress!&nbsp; Loki travels to see her, knowing that, despite her incredible beauty, she is cruel and ambitious.&nbsp; He presents her with the challenge of going to Earth and winning the heart of Don Blake.&nbsp; She accepts, \"for long have I wished to conquer the heart of handsome Thor!\"&nbsp; Loki knows that teaming up with the Enchantress would make them <i>quite</i> a formidable team, but they're too much alike and could never trust each other.\n\nSoon, the Enchantress arrives in New York and demands a wardrobe be made for her.&nbsp; She then goes to see Don Blake (who recognizes her) and manipulates him so that Jane walks into the room just as the Enchantress kisses him.&nbsp; Jane storms out with Don hobbling after her.&nbsp; Enchantress is astonished at Thor's strength of character to resist her and returns to Asgard.&nbsp; She requires the aid of someone who can eliminate the mortal girl from the equation -- the Executioner!&nbsp; He has always loved her, and she asks him to go to Earth and make sure that Don Blake is never able to set eyes on Jane Foster again!\n\nDon Blake turns to Thor to help in his search for Jane.&nbsp; He even barges into the police commissioner's office to request help in locating her, but he has a more pressing matter, as there's suddenly an 8-foot tall guy with a battle axe spreading panic.&nbsp; Thor recognizes the description of the Executioner, who is out scanning the crowd for Jane...and <i>finds</i> her!&nbsp; He uses his battle axe to trap her in \"<i>Limbo</i>, the half-world between here and there!\"\n\nThor arrives in time to see Jane vanish and engages the Executioner.&nbsp; They battle for a couple of pages before the Executioner offers a deal.&nbsp; Thor will surrender his hammer to him, and in return, he will restore Jane.&nbsp; Thor reluctantly agrees, and Jane is restored.&nbsp; Thor hands him the hammer, only for the Executioner to discover that he cannot lift it.&nbsp; What <i>good</i> is it to him then?\n\nBut suddenly, the voice of the Enchantress speaks, angry that her ally has made a deal instead of removing the girl as commissioned.&nbsp; She uses her sorcery to turn his limbs into tree branches, and the Enchantress then appears.&nbsp; The Executioner realizes that he needs Thor's help, and the hammer is restored to him (all of this occurring within the 60 second time limit).\n\nThe Enchantress then threatens to turn his hammer into a serpent, but Thor reminds her that the hammer was \"forged by Odin himself.&nbsp; No power in the universe, save <i>his</i>, can affect this enchanted weapon!\"&nbsp; And Thor reminds her that her beauty too can be resisted by \"the heart of a god\" (especially one in love with <i>another</i>).&nbsp; Thor then whirls his hammer around and banishes them -- back to Asgard.\n\nSoon after, Don Blake catches up with Jane, who icily informs him that she's now waiting for Thor (hoping to make him jealous).&nbsp; All the while, Odin stares in amazement from above, not believing that they're <i>still</i> in love.&nbsp; He's <i>done</i> sitting around!\n\n\"By Asgard, my son may <i>not</i> love a mortal!&nbsp; Thor may not defy his father <i>Odin</i>!!&nbsp; The time for words is past!&nbsp; Now I must <i>act</i>!!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "An uneventful story introduces far more interesting ideas than it actually explores.\nNew faces and a growing Asgard supply the sparks the plot itself lacks.",
  "subtitle": "Asgard Expands Its Roster... Eventually",
  "review_text": "To start off with, we're never told if Thor's full power was restored to him (it having been halved a couple of issues ago).&nbsp; No mention of it was made at the conclusion of the previous issue (which I pointed out), and the only <i>hint</i> this issue gives occurs on page 2, where the narration mentions that after Thor's return from the 23<sup>rd</sup> century, \"the almost indescribable power surges back into his massive, ripping muscles.\"\n\n[HANG0] That's... <i>nice</i>... but it doesn't actually address the elephant in the room:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Is his full power restored or not?]\n\nThe reader must simply <b>assume</b> that it is, but I look forward to seeing if there are any signs which indicate if a particularly astute <i>contemporary</i> reader ever questioned this in a letters page.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Moving on now...)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ExhaustedBlake | width=11%] [ASSET: right | AsgardTransition | width=13%] After Thor reverts to the mortal Don Blake and collapses in his office, Jane enters and helps him to a couch.&nbsp; His mind is then flooded with images from last month's encounter with Zarrko, the Tomorrow Man.&nbsp; Only when we finally reach page 4 does Kirby transition over to Asgard.\n[BR]\n[BR] I found this a <i>remarkably</i> inefficient use of space, especially in a story that spans only 13 pages.\n[BR] (The splash page's pronouncement of a \"feature-length\" adventure [SPAN: severity=error; text=is nothing but a bold-faced <b>lie</b>!])\n\n[HANG0] To Stan and Jack:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=You have limited space, and you essentially wait until page 4 to begin this month's adventure?]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=small; fonttype=bold-italic; text=(Ugh!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhen they finally get on with the new story, it's amazing that, not only does Odin immediately grasp Loki's idea of finding Thor a more beautiful woman, [SPAN: severity=error; text=he's actually okay with the fact that it was Loki's idea!]\n\nYou'd think by now, he'd know that Loki is up to no good, so you'd also wonder why, whenever Loki suggests something that <b>seems</b> to be in line with what Odin wants... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=he just <i>might</i> want to look under the hood of the plan before agreeing to it!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=But <i>no</i>...] Odin is happy to sit on his throne and stew over his son's choice of mates [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Doesn't he have anything <i>better</i> to do, like... I don't know... <i>rule a kingdom</i> perhaps?)], but that doesn't stop him from commissioning his evil step-son by essentially saying [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Sure Loki, that sounds like a <i>great</i> idea!&nbsp; Go off and take care of it!\"]\n\nDoes Loki's track record up to this point count for <i>nothing</i>?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(shakes head)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAfter Loki recruits the Enchantress, the next couple of pages are all about her trip to see the mortal doctor, where she makes sure that Jane Foster sees them kissing.&nbsp; (At least Blake finally recognizes her.)\n[ASSET: center | FindHisLips | width=large]\n\nWhen Blake is horrified at the sight of Jane's shock, it only illustrates how much Don loves her.&nbsp; And the Enchantress can't have <i>that</i> -- she wants to win Thor's affection, so [SPAN: severity=error; text=Jane must be eliminated!]\n\n[ASSET: left | Executioner | width=12%] [ASSET: right | JaneSentToLimbo | width=10%] <b>Enter the Executioner</b>, who seems willing to do <i>anything</i> for the Enchantress.&nbsp; He travels to Earth and hunts down the human female, ultimately sending Jane to Limbo (as opposed to downright killing her).\n[BR]\n[BR] My only question is:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Where is the Space Phantom?]\n\nIf you'll recall the ending of last September's [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08], the Space Phantom was thrown into Limbo himself after he tried to take on Thor's shape (not being able to affect immortals), and Giant-Man said that he would \"have to <i>remain </i>in Limbo until someone comes to <i>replace</i> him!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=That wasn't <i>Jane</i>?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I'm confused... but let's not overthink things...)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | CantLiftIt | width=12%] To wrap things up, Thor rushes to battle the Executioner (providing Jack the perfect excuse to draw a couple of pages of fisticuffs), Thor agrees to give his opponent his hammer if the Executioner returns Jane.&nbsp; The agreement is struck, Jane returns, and <i>of course</i>, [SPAN: severity=info; text=the Executioner finds he can't lift the hammer] (a plot point anyone could have seen coming a mile away).\n\nWhat's interesting about this sequence isn't the agreement, or the Executioner's idiocy in not immediately spotting the flaw in his logic.&nbsp; It's that he seems to have <i>completely </i>forgotten why he was hired -- [SPAN: severity=error; text=to eliminate Jane] -- which the Enchantress is quick to remind him, ultimately leading to the poor oaf releasing Thor from his word and returning his hammer.\n\n[ASSET: right | ForgedByOdin | width=22%] The Enchantress threatens to turn the hammer into a snake on page 12, but Thor reminds her that his hammer was \"forged by Odin himself\" (an assertion that is <i>repeated</i> in this issue's [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Tales of Asgard] installment).\n[BR]\n[BR] That strikes me as surprising.\n[HANG] Did Odin actually forge such a weapon?\n[HANG] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Doesn't he have <b><i>people</i></b> for that?]\n[HANG] I'm sure the hammer's <i>enchantment</i> likely came from Odin... but actual <i>forging</i>?\n\n<i>Hmm</i>... I'll definitely want to keep an eye on this going forward, but at the very least, it prevented the Enchantress from turning it into a serpent.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nSeeing that gaining Thor's affection is hopeless, the Enchantress backs down.&nbsp; This, too, is interesting, because <i>twice</i> during this first appearance, she has demonstrated remarkable pragmatism.&nbsp; Unlike many villains, she doesn't keep escalating after she's clearly lost.&nbsp; She simply goes off and tries another plan.\n\nThor banishes both of them back to Asgard (where the Enchantress will doubtless stew over this defeat... and perhaps <i>return</i>).\n[ASSET: center | BanishedToAsgard | width=large]\n\nThat leaves time for Jane and Don Blake to have a final moment together (more or less).\n\n[ASSET: left | UnhappyOdin | width=14%] But if they think they're going to somehow make it work, it's a good thing they didn't see Odin's face just then.&nbsp; The formula (now three issues old) of Odin staring at the action from above, looking to others to get his son's eyes off the mortal Jane Foster, seems to finally be broken, and Odin apparently is going to get involved directly!\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Don't Miss Next Issue!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nI must say that the cover was quite impressive with Thor battling the two new foes on Bifrost, the Rainbow bridge against the backdrop of planets and outer space in general.&nbsp; I don't recall a cover with that type of grandiose setting.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Even the far more famous <i>Silver Surfer</i> #4 cover doesn't have that much background depicted.&nbsp; This issue's cover also was very reminiscent of the 20-cent 1970's \"box\" covers.]\n\nYet... it is at least worth mentioning that the scene depicted on the cover [SPAN: severity=warning; text=never actually takes <i>place</i> in this story!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(creative license at its <i>finest</i>, I guess)].\n\n[ASSET: left | UndersoldBeauty | width=12%] From an art perspective, as hard as Jack Kirby tries, his rendition of the Enchantress, [SPAN: severity=success; text=though <i>good</i>], nevertheless undersells her looks, especially one (as the narration states on page 4) \"whose beauty is deemed to be more than any mortal man can resist!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] If this panel is any indicator, it only proves that [SPAN: severity=info; text=Jack Kirby's strengths lie elsewhere...]\n[BR] (By the same token, it remains true that Kirby's dynamic battle action is not to be <i>missed</i>!)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nWhen it comes right down to it, the story was functionally uneventful, but still contained the obligatory couple of action pages, for which the Executioner was perhaps invented -- precisely the antagonist the story needed.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=I mean, come <i>on</i> -- did you expect Thor to punch a <i>goddess</i>?]&nbsp; That would hardly seem right, so perhaps Stan and Jack brought him in to give Thor someone to trade punches with -- <b>this is Jack Kirby after all</b> -- there <i>has</i> to be <i>someone</i> duking it out every time!\n\nYet the issue also provided more details about Asgard than we readers had <i>ever</i> been given before.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=It implies an actual population, with people living lives beyond Odin's court and Thor's adventures.]&nbsp; It's [SPAN: severity=success; text=very nice] to see a new realm beginning to take shape -- and know that it's more than just a mystical place where people sit around twiddling their thumbs and staring at what's happening on Earth.\n\nThe fact that both Loki <i>and</i> the Enchantress had to visit other parts of the realm in order to [SPAN: severity=info; text=enter the actual homes of their respective recruits] suggests a far larger Asgard than these pages have ever hinted at before (outside of <i>Tales of Asgard</i>).\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=This was great to see, and it helps generate interest in the fabled realm, so I'm all for it!]\n\nThe manufactured battle action and the fact that Stan and Jack spent <i>way</i> too much time getting this month's tale on its feet were negatives.&nbsp; Stalling -- and never addressing Thor's power level -- strikes me as a [SPAN: severity=error; text=weak way to begin a story.] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(You guys are <i>better</i> than this!)]\n\n[HANG0] Even though it wasn't captivating -- even downright <i>lightweight</i> at times\n[BR] -- the new ingredients being added to the series <b>feel important.</b>\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=That's <i>more</i> than enough to bring people back next month.]",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of Asgard:</b><i> </i>\"Thor's Mission to Mirmir!\"\n\nThor arrives in the mountains of Asgard, where King Sindri and the dwarfs live, where all the enchanted weapons of Asgard are forged.&nbsp; Here Thor is given a magic ship that flies through the air to take Thor wherever he needs to go.&nbsp; He heads to Mirmir, where he encounters a couple of perils (and once again indicates his \"hammer was forged by Odin himself\" -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=<i>curious</i>, especially since he just said a couple of pages earlier that dwarves were the ones that forged all their weapons]).&nbsp; Finally Thor approaches the one he seeks and hands him a branch \"from Yggdrasill, the tree of life!\" saying \"You know what must be done!\"\n\nWhen King Mirmir stirs the branch into some enchanted waters, two figures begin to take shape in Midgard below:&nbsp; the shapes of Aske and Embla, \"destined to start a new race, in the image of the immortals of Asgard!\"&nbsp; Thor heads home in his new magical ship, ready to be assigned his next task by Odin!\n\nWe learn in the Editor's Note that this tale was part of the Norse legends \"which deal with the birth of mankind and the days before the beginning of time.\"&nbsp; Next issue will begin a series of biographies about different Asgardians, with Heimdall first on the upcoming list!"
  ],
  "highlight": "Thor gains two memorable new foes, but the real surprise is discovering that Asgard extends beyond Odin's throne room."
}